  the reward. The skill pattern is `set -uo pipefail`, `|| true` after
  the test runner, then decide 1 or 0 from the real status.

Factory overlay (stricter than the demos; do not copy demo `test.sh`):

- It runs after the agent, inside the task environment, as root, with no
  guaranteed working directory. Use absolute paths everywhere.
- It writes exactly `1` or `0` to `/logs/verifier/reward.txt` on every exit
  path (binary reward, no partial credit) and exits non-zero on failure. Write
  the reward last, from the final verdict; never pre-write `1`.
- It is deterministic and bounded in time. `test.sh` may install
  test-only dependencies with pinned versions, but installation failure must
  produce reward `0`; do not use `curl | sh` or unpinned `latest` packages.
  Task/runtime dependencies still belong in the base environment or overlay.
- Tools available before `test.sh` are POSIX `bash` / coreutils, this
  candidate'"'"'s `oracle_tools` from `05_task_design.json`, and 03 `entrypoints`.
  A dependency installed successfully by `test.sh` may then be invoked by the
  verifier; record its exact package and version in the verifier handoff.
  If `oracle_tools` includes `python3`, use stdlib (`json`, `hashlib`,
  `pathlib`) unless a pinned test dependency is explicitly installed.
- It verifies behavior by executing or reading the agent'"'"'s outputs, not by
  grepping source files or shell history for keywords.
- If it executes agent-produced code, `test.sh` derives the reward from the
  child'"'"'s exit status and output; executed code must never write the reward.
- Use pytest, CTRF, or another test framework only when `test.sh` installs a
  pinned version before invoking it. Otherwise use bash plus the listed
  `oracle_tools` and 03 `entrypoints`.
- Skill Example 1 (`assert fib(10) == 55` inline) and Pattern 2 (file
